'\" t
.TH OBJDUMP01 1 "October 2026" "mc6801-tools" "mc6801-tools Manual"
.SH NAME
objdump01, nm01, size01 \- display information about MC6801 object files and executables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B objdump01
.RB [ \-n | \-s ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-o ]
.I file
\&...
.br
.B nm01
.RB [ \-o ]
.I file
\&...
.br
.B size01
.I file
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B objdump01
displays the contents of object files produced by
.BR as01 (1),
archives of them made by
.BR ar01 (1),
and a.out executables produced by
.BR ld01 (1).
.B make install
also installs it as
.B nm01
and
.BR size01 ,
which behave as if
.B \-n
or
.B \-s
had been given.
The mode is chosen from the first letter of the program name.
.PP
By default
.B objdump01
prints for each executable:
.IP \(bu 2
the header: header length, CPU (m6801), flags, entry point
.RB ( a_entry ),
total memory size
.RB ( a_total )
and symbol table size;
.IP \(bu
the segment sizes;
.IP \(bu
the symbol table;
.IP \(bu
a hex and ASCII dump of the text and data segments.
.PP
For each object file, or each object file in an archive, it prints:
.IP \(bu 2
the module name and the size of each location counter
.RB ( SEG0 ,
.BR SEG3 ,
\&...);
.IP \(bu
the symbol table, with flags for exported
.RB ( E ),
imported
.RB ( I ),
entry point
.RB ( N )
and common
.RB ( C )
symbols;
.IP \(bu
the text, data and bss sizes;
.IP \(bu
the module\(aqs code, with the relocation records that
.BR ld01 (1)
uses to fill in addresses, such as
.B "DW clrscn"
for a 2\-byte reference to the symbol
.BR clrscn .
.PP
In object files, location counters 0 and 1 count as text and the
others as data, as they are linked by
.BR ld01 (1).
.PP
With
.BR \-d ,
the code is also disassembled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
After the rest of the output, disassemble the code: an executable\(aqs
text segment, at the address of its
.B __btext
symbol, and location counters 0 and 1 of each object module, from
offset 0.
Each line gives the address, the instruction\(aqs bytes, a label if a
symbol is defined there, the mnemonic and the operand, in
.BR as01 (1)
syntax.
In object files, an operand that
.BR ld01 (1)
fills in is shown by name: the imported symbol, or the label nearest
before the address in the module (such as
.BR local+$2 ),
with
.B <
for a direct address and
.B #
for an immediate.
In executables, addresses and branch targets are shown by name where a
symbol has that value.
Bytes that aren\(aqt an instruction are shown with
.BR fcb ,
a relocated field that isn\(aqt an operand with
.BR fdb ,
and an area that wasn\(aqt loaded (\fBRMB\fP) with
.BR rmb .
.TP
.B \-n
Print only the symbol table, like
.BR nm (1).
Each line gives the value, a type letter and the name.
.TP
.B \-s
Print only the text, data and bss sizes, in decimal, followed by the total
in decimal and hexadecimal, like
.BR size (1).
If several files are given, a total line is added.
.TP
.B \-o
In
.B \-n
mode, prefix each line with the file name instead of printing a
heading for each file.
.SH SYMBOL TYPES
.TS
l l.
\fBT\fP, \fBt\fP	text segment symbol (global, local)
\fBD\fP, \fBd\fP	data segment symbol (global, local)
\fBC\fP, \fBb\fP	bss or common symbol (global, local)
\fBa\fP	absolute symbol
\fBU\fP	undefined
\fB?\fP	other
.TE
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.B $ size01 main.aout
text	data	bss	dec	hex	filename
108	0	0	108	6c	main.aout
.sp
.B $ nm01 main.aout
0000434c t msg_one
00004351 T one
00004380 T __start
00004000 a SCREEN
\&...
.sp
.B $ nm01 main.o
00000000 T main
         U three
         U one
\&...
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors, such as a file that can\(aqt be opened or isn\(aqt an object
file or executable, are reported and the next file is examined.
In the default mode they are printed on standard output, as
.BI "Error: " message\fR,
among the rest of the output;
.B nm01
and
.B size01
print them on standard error.
.PP
The exit status is 0 if every file could be read, and 1 if there was
an error or no file was given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ld01 (1),
.BR objcopy01 (1),
.BR as01 (1)
.PP
The mc6801\-tools User\(aqs Manual
.RI ( mc6801\-tools\-manual.pdf ).
.SH BUGS
The disassembler can\(aqt tell data from code: data in a code segment,
such as a string, is shown as instructions where its bytes happen to be
opcodes.
.PP
Symbol names in executables are cut to 8 characters.
.PP
Unknown options are silently ignored.
.SH HISTORY
Derived from
.B objdump86
in the dev86 package, by Greg Haerr and others.
